## Asymmetry, published rather than buried
  The windowed char count is a FLOOR on the request: system prompt, tools and carried
  context are unmodelled here and can only ADD input tokens. So a TRIGGERS verdict off
  this floor is sound; a DOES-NOT-TRIGGER verdict only says "not from message content
  alone" and can be overturned by the unmodelled additions. Only a live call with a key
  returns the real input_tokens — which is the blocked part of the capability test.

## SUMMARY
  The repo's one reachable corpus is 3860602 bytes and yields 132
  history-eligible messages. The request carries at most the last 200 of them.
  Whole channel: ~43794–65691 tokens (DOES-NOT-TRIGGER).
  What is actually sent: ~43794–65691 tokens (DOES-NOT-TRIGGER) against a 160000 trigger.

  => The in-repo corpus is NOT large enough once the window is applied. "Bring a big
     channel" is the wrong instruction twice over: the binding quantity is message
     CONTENT in the last 200 messages, and here the window does not even bite.
     Content needed, as a band: >= 720000 chars GUARANTEES the trigger at the
     sparse end of the bracket; < 480000 chars guarantees it is NOT reached on
     content alone; between the two is undetermined without a live call.
     This corpus has 197073 — short of the lower figure by
     2.4x, and of the upper by 3.7x.
     Floor caveat above applies: the unmodelled additions can only help.
